WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.722
Hey everyone, welcome back to mCoding. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:02.722 --> 00:00:06.080
Today we're talking about the two argument form of iter().

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.252
That's right. iter() can actually take two arguments.

00:00:08.252 --> 00:00:10.740
And I bet you've never used the two argument form.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.515
Don't worry, though. You weren't missing much.

00:00:12.515 --> 00:00:14.639
First up, though, the one argument form.

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:16.356
If you pass in one argument to iter(),

00:00:16.356 --> 00:00:20.263
all it does is call that argument's dunder iter method.

00:00:20.263 --> 00:00:22.041
If you've used Python for more than a few months,

00:00:22.041 --> 00:00:24.049
you've probably already come across this.

00:00:24.049 --> 00:00:27.831
Dunder iter allows you to customize what it means to iterate over your class.

00:00:27.831 --> 00:00:29.904
In this case, my class is a LucasSequence.

00:00:29.904 --> 00:00:32.854
It's a generalization of a fibonacci sequence.

00:00:32.854 --> 00:00:38.079
You define the next term in the sequence as p times the previous one minus q times the one before that.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:40.662
If you're familiar with the linear way to compute the fibonacci sequence,

00:00:40.662 --> 00:00:43.040
that's exactly what this code is doing.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.719
And in fact, by choosing the right initial values and constants, we can actually create the fibonacci sequence.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:50.997
We grab out the iterator and every time we call next,

00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:53.271
we get another number of the sequence.

00:00:53.271 --> 00:00:56.681
So, when we run the code, down here at the bottom we see 0 1 1 2 3.

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:58.792
That's the start of the fibonacci sequence.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:02.640
Of course, the one arg form of iter() is deeply connected to how for-loops work.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.738
If I do for n in fib, then it's going to call the one arg form of iter()

00:01:05.738 --> 00:01:10.080
to get the iterator and then repeatedly call next putting that value into n.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.816
And this is probably the main reason why you've never used the two arg form of iter()

00:01:13.816 --> 00:01:17.511
Because normally, you don't call it or directly. You just use a for-loop.

00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:19.363
So, what does the two arg iter() do?

00:01:19.363 --> 00:01:21.759
Well, it's mostly equivalent to this code.

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:24.746
The first thing to note is that although it does return an iterator,

00:01:24.746 --> 00:01:28.799
that's basically where the similarity with the one arg form of iter() ends.

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:31.526
It very confusingly does something completely different.

00:01:31.526 --> 00:01:34.799
It doesn't even take in an iterable for its first argument.

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:38.799
Instead, its first argument needs to be a callable, not an iterable.

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:41.476
And no surprise, it's going to call that callable.

00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:46.479
So, this version of iter() is calling dunder call, not dunder iter.

00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:50.505
Whenever you ask it for its next value, it will call the given callable with no arguments.

00:01:50.505 --> 00:01:52.814
If the value equals the sentinel, then we return

00:01:52.814 --> 00:01:56.079
which ultimately leads to a StopIteration being raised.

00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:58.018
Otherwise, we yield that value.

00:01:58.018 --> 00:02:00.000
So, if you've never heard of this word sentinel before,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.363
you can also think of it just as a stop token.

00:02:02.363 --> 00:02:04.479
Keep calling the function f yielding its values

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:07.386
until you see the sentinel or the stop token.

00:02:07.386 --> 00:02:09.758
And then that signals the end of the process.

00:02:09.758 --> 00:02:11.892
So, how might you use this two argument form?

00:02:11.892 --> 00:02:16.413
Well, actually, passing in a function is not going to be that helpful.

00:02:16.413 --> 00:02:19.326
If I have a pure function as most of your functions should be,

00:02:19.326 --> 00:02:20.939
then look what happens.

00:02:20.939 --> 00:02:25.027
Let's suppose that my sentinel is not 42. In this case, I'll just use None.

00:02:25.027 --> 00:02:28.260
Well, I'm supposed to keep calling the function until I hit the sentinel None

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.868
which is never going to happen. It always returns 42.

00:02:30.868 --> 00:02:34.400
So, every time I call next, it's just going to return 42 again.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.654
Okay. So, that's pretty useless.

00:02:36.654 --> 00:02:41.703
Or, if I did put in 42, then I just immediately get a stop iteration because that's the sentinel.

00:02:41.703 --> 00:02:43.437
So, that's also pretty useless.

00:02:43.437 --> 00:02:46.811
So, what we've discovered is that this is useless for pure functions.

00:02:46.811 --> 00:02:51.730
But if you rewind, I actually said that it takes a callable, not necessarily a function.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.848
So, it's pretty much necessary that every time you call this callable,

00:02:54.848 --> 00:02:57.916
it changes some kind of global or internal state.

00:02:57.916 --> 00:03:01.787
That way, the next time it comes around and calls it, something else can happen.

00:03:01.787 --> 00:03:04.109
The most common kind of state mutating callable

00:03:04.109 --> 00:03:08.760
that you're going to run into in practice is just a class that defines a dunder call.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.139
So, let's take a look at one of those examples.

00:03:11.139 --> 00:03:15.831
The goal of this ChunkedReader is to allow you to read from a file in chunks of given size.

00:03:15.831 --> 00:03:17.732
f is our file handle.

00:03:17.732 --> 00:03:19.133
And every time we call this object,

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:22.315
we just read that many bytes from the file and return the result.

00:03:22.315 --> 00:03:24.265
And note that every time we call read on a file,

00:03:24.265 --> 00:03:26.694
this advances where we're reading from in the file.

00:03:26.694 --> 00:03:30.414
So, eventually if we call this enough times, we'll get to the end of the file.

00:03:30.414 --> 00:03:32.981
And when that happens, it'll return either empty bytes

00:03:32.981 --> 00:03:36.418
or the empty string depending on whether you're in binary mode.

00:03:36.418 --> 00:03:38.229
So, we could tell that we're at the end of the file.

00:03:38.229 --> 00:03:41.346
And that we want to stop when we see that empty bytes or empty string.

00:03:41.346 --> 00:03:43.425
So, that will be our sentinel value.

00:03:43.425 --> 00:03:45.546
Then we could use the ChunkedReader like this.

00:03:45.546 --> 00:03:47.625
We'll open up some example file.

00:03:47.625 --> 00:03:50.617
We'll make a ChunkedReader that reads from the file 4 bytes at a time.

00:03:50.617 --> 00:03:53.758
4 is really small but it's just for demonstration purposes.

00:03:53.758 --> 00:03:58.319
Then this says: Keep reading through the chunks until we hit the empty bytes sentinel value.

00:03:58.319 --> 00:03:59.616
Here's the file I'm reading from.

00:03:59.616 --> 00:04:03.580
It's just going to be 123456789 all the way up to 20.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.413
And when we run it, you can see '1234' '5678'. We get chunks of 4.

00:04:08.413 --> 00:04:13.040
So, technically this works. But there's a few problems with the design.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.639
First off, I had to create this ChunkedReader class in the first place.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:19.279
That probably shouldn't be necessary.

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:23.076
Secondly, it's kind of a bad choice to call this method dunder call.

00:04:23.076 --> 00:04:27.134
Now, we had to do that in order to make it work with the two argument iter().

00:04:27.134 --> 00:04:29.672
But it should really be called something like read_chunk.

00:04:29.672 --> 00:04:34.588
Normally, it's a bad idea to define something like dunder call just to allow for some special syntax,

00:04:34.588 --> 00:04:37.116
unless that syntax actually makes sense.

00:04:37.116 --> 00:04:42.016
If I was defining some kind of function proxy object where it would make sense to call a function,

00:04:42.016 --> 00:04:45.134
then defining a dunder call might very well make sense.

00:04:45.134 --> 00:04:48.075
But for this ChunkedReader, I don't think so.

00:04:48.075 --> 00:04:50.528
So, if I really want to make the two argument form make sense,

00:04:50.528 --> 00:04:54.508
then I need to pass in a class that kind of models function semantics.

00:04:54.508 --> 00:04:59.158
Pretty much the only realistic example of this that you would run into is funktools partial.

00:04:59.158 --> 00:05:03.327
This takes in a callable and some arguments to pre-fill for that callable.

00:05:03.327 --> 00:05:06.365
And you can then call that callable at some later time.

00:05:06.365 --> 00:05:08.148
This example is the same as the previous one.

00:05:08.148 --> 00:05:11.907
But it allows you to avoid having to make that intermediate class.

00:05:11.907 --> 00:05:14.496
Of course, though now, you have this monstrosity in your code.

00:05:14.496 --> 00:05:16.852
So, is that really much better?

00:05:16.852 --> 00:05:18.630
Well, I think it's a little bit better.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:22.525
But in my opinion, this two argument form of iter() was a mistake.

00:05:22.525 --> 00:05:24.635
I think that if you really wanted this behavior,

00:05:24.635 --> 00:05:26.593
it should be a function in the iter tools library,

00:05:26.593 --> 00:05:31.452
not a completely separate and different functionality in the built-in iter.

00:05:31.452 --> 00:05:32.480
So, stop using it.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.960
Or more likely, since you weren't already using it, just keep doing what you're doing.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:42.174
If it were up to me, I would deprecate this functionality and eventually remove it from the language.

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:43.865
And I don't feel bad about suggesting this.

00:05:43.865 --> 00:05:47.789
Because as of Python 3.8, there is a much better alternative.

00:05:47.789 --> 00:05:51.943
Python 3.8 introduced the walrus operator and assignment expressions.

00:05:51.943 --> 00:05:55.948
Assignment expressions allow you to assign a variable and use it at the same time.

00:05:55.948 --> 00:05:58.463
You get to have your cake and eat it too.

00:05:58.463 --> 00:06:03.000
Every iteration of the loop, I can call my function however I want to and capture its value.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.020
Since I capture the value here, it now becomes available to use within the loop.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.360
But an assignment expression also returns the value back.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.293
So, I can continue using it and compare it to the sentinel value.

00:06:13.293 --> 00:06:17.759
In my opinion, this code is easier to read and the intent is much clearer.

00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:21.754
Additionally, this gives you a lot more flexibility on how to control when to stop.

00:06:21.754 --> 00:06:23.736
Here, I'm checking if the chunk is equal to the sentinel.

00:06:23.736 --> 00:06:26.113
But I could also check if the chunk is the sentinel,

00:06:26.113 --> 00:06:27.735
or if it's close to the sentinel,

00:06:27.735 --> 00:06:31.007
or any other condition involving the chunk and the sentinel.

00:06:31.007 --> 00:06:32.807
For example, here I take a starting time.

00:06:32.807 --> 00:06:35.873
And then every iteration of the loop, I get the current time.

00:06:35.873 --> 00:06:39.308
This condition basically says keep looping until it's been one second.

00:06:39.308 --> 00:06:43.041
Of course, I'm not going to hit exactly one second later. There's going to be some error.

00:06:43.041 --> 00:06:46.814
So, I can't just check for t0+1 as a sentinel value.

00:06:46.814 --> 00:06:49.689
But I can check whether or not I've exceeded that value.

00:06:49.689 --> 00:06:52.829
So, that's an example where the two argument iter just wouldn't work.

00:06:52.829 --> 00:06:55.490
In summary, the two argument form of iter exists.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:58.413
Don't use it. Instead, use assignment expressions.

